WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.962
For VR, I had experiences with two labs

00:00:02.962 --> 00:00:05.302
I wanted to do this kind of hard super-realistic

00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:07.012
experience which was the

00:00:07.012 --> 00:00:09.772
military experience where they really

00:00:09.772 --> 00:00:12.572
try to simulate what it's like to be in combat.

00:00:12.832 --> 00:00:15.892
So in the travel lab I saw we just

00:00:16.032 --> 00:00:18.652
wore goggles. But if you went into their real lab

00:00:18.652 --> 00:00:20.552
they would they would pump in smells

00:00:20.552 --> 00:00:22.212
they would vibrate the floor to

00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:25.492
emulate kind of driving motor shaking

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:27.442
explosions and stuff like that. And then

00:00:27.442 --> 00:00:28.792
I went to this lab at Stanford called the

00:00:28.792 --> 00:00:30.532
Virtual Human Interaction Lab which is

00:00:30.532 --> 00:00:34.251
like the magic realism side of the VR

00:00:34.251 --> 00:00:35.812
world and they're really inspired by

00:00:35.812 --> 00:00:37.522
Jaron Lanier and kind of this idea of

00:00:37.522 --> 00:00:39.981
alternate bodies. So one of the things that

00:00:39.981 --> 00:00:41.751
I did in there--they never told me

00:00:41.751 --> 00:00:43.132
what I was going to do they just said to

00:00:43.132 --> 00:00:45.172
the lab. And I got there one day and they

00:00:45.172 --> 00:00:46.882
said get down on all fours and wear

00:00:46.882 --> 00:00:49.222
these infrared markers. They put them on

00:00:49.222 --> 00:00:50.661
my back and they put them on my arm.

00:00:50.661 --> 00:00:54.051
OK fine. And they snapped on the VR and

00:00:54.051 --> 00:00:58.792
I was a cow. Right? And I was like... okay.

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:00.322
What they're experimenting with is that

00:01:00.322 --> 00:01:02.392
Jaron Lanier kind of famously asked this

00:01:02.392 --> 00:01:04.402
question of why do you have to have a

00:01:04.402 --> 00:01:07.192
human body in virtual reality?

00:01:07.192 --> 00:01:09.652
He said imagine that you're a lobster

00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:11.692
ok you drive arm one with this arm

00:01:11.692 --> 00:01:13.131
drive two with this arm. How do you

00:01:13.131 --> 00:01:15.262
drive the rest of the arms? So the

00:01:15.262 --> 00:01:17.002
question is how well can you adapt

00:01:17.002 --> 00:01:18.652
to these novel bodies. They put me in a cow

00:01:18.652 --> 00:01:21.262
and I could see my cow doppelganger

00:01:21.262 --> 00:01:22.372
across from me because it's actually

00:01:22.372 --> 00:01:23.782
very hard to wear a helmet and crawl

00:01:23.782 --> 00:01:25.102
around on the floor and look at your own

00:01:25.102 --> 00:01:27.652
body at the cow body. So they did

00:01:27.652 --> 00:01:28.881
all of these things that kind of bonded

00:01:28.881 --> 00:01:30.741
me to the cow. Do this

00:01:30.741 --> 00:01:32.692
and then cow would do that. And then they

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:34.341
said okay here's your job you have to

00:01:34.341 --> 00:01:36.292
gain all of this weight so you reach a

00:01:36.292 --> 00:01:37.372
certain number of pounds.

00:01:37.372 --> 00:01:40.671
So I go over and I eat. I go over

00:01:40.671 --> 00:01:42.982
and I drink. And then they say go back to

00:01:42.982 --> 00:01:44.091
your starting point because now we're

00:01:44.091 --> 00:01:45.262
going to send you to the slaughterhouse.

00:01:45.262 --> 00:01:50.332
Right? And then the floor starts to shake

00:01:50.332 --> 00:01:53.722
and this truck like it starts making that

00:01:53.722 --> 00:01:56.811
beep beep beep back up noises and I

00:01:56.811 --> 00:02:00.112
freaked out. I can hear myself I

00:02:00.112 --> 00:02:02.691
have a tape of myself and in it I'm

00:02:02.691 --> 00:02:05.542
yelling "This is brutal" like at

00:02:05.542 --> 00:02:06.331
no one in particular

00:02:06.331 --> 00:02:07.742
and VR is like being on stage

00:02:07.742 --> 00:02:09.182
you're not supposed to break the

00:02:09.182 --> 00:02:11.671
illusion you know of the fourth wall. I am

00:02:11.671 --> 00:02:13.712
really scared. And it's because basically

00:02:13.712 --> 00:02:16.442
I have come to empathize with

00:02:16.442 --> 00:02:19.412
this other that quickly. Like I

00:02:19.412 --> 00:02:21.692
feel so responsible for this other

00:02:21.692 --> 00:02:23.762
imaginary being and that turns out to be

00:02:23.762 --> 00:02:25.712
what they were trying to test. In the

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:26.701
real experiment what they would have

00:02:26.701 --> 00:02:28.561
done afterwards is kind of put me

00:02:28.561 --> 00:02:30.841
through all of these batteries of tests

00:02:30.841 --> 00:02:32.641
to gauge how I felt about animals and

00:02:32.641 --> 00:02:34.681
how likely i was to donate to animal

00:02:34.681 --> 00:02:37.111
rights causes. And that was like

00:02:37.111 --> 00:02:39.001
a real experience. It was very visceral.

00:02:39.001 --> 00:02:40.651
And the other things that they did after

00:02:40.651 --> 00:02:42.361
that was that they made me a coral because

00:02:42.361 --> 00:02:44.611
they wanted to see how people would

00:02:44.611 --> 00:02:46.412
behave in a body that is even more like

00:02:46.412 --> 00:02:49.322
yourself. And then

00:02:49.322 --> 00:02:50.431
they did a thing where they gave

00:02:50.431 --> 00:02:52.681
me a third arm. And the idea was how

00:02:52.681 --> 00:02:54.592
quickly would I learn how to drive a

00:02:54.592 --> 00:02:57.672
third arm. And I'll tell you it happened like that.

00:02:58.012 --> 00:03:02.332
[inaudible question...] Ok so... in VR

00:03:03.112 --> 00:03:06.512
the third arm is coming out of my chest

00:03:06.512 --> 00:03:08.072
and I'm wearing controllers on my wrist.

00:03:08.072 --> 00:03:09.781
They didn't tell me how to drive it.

00:03:09.781 --> 00:03:11.852
They just said make your third arm move.

00:03:11.852 --> 00:03:14.611
They said tap that thing. It was just like that.

00:03:14.611 --> 00:03:17.132
It was an amazing experience.

00:03:17.132 --> 00:03:20.372
Yeah. Did I answer all the...?

00:03:21.212 --> 00:03:29.252
("What's next for virtual reality?")

00:03:31.012 --> 00:03:33.292
Both of these labs were trying to do things

00:03:33.292 --> 00:03:35.632
for basically for pro-social reasons to help

00:03:35.642 --> 00:03:38.042
people. To either prevent a

00:03:38.042 --> 00:03:39.902
disorder cure disorder or kind of make

00:03:39.902 --> 00:03:41.132
people more empathetic with their

00:03:41.132 --> 00:03:43.142
environment and with other people.

00:03:43.142 --> 00:03:45.331
I thought that was a really like awesome

00:03:45.331 --> 00:03:47.281
application of VR. They were

00:03:47.281 --> 00:03:49.142
talking about using it with students.

00:03:49.142 --> 00:03:50.852
Could you bring students in

00:03:50.852 --> 00:03:53.312
and instead of reading about global warming

00:03:53.312 --> 00:03:55.922
and the effect on ocean life would you

00:03:55.922 --> 00:03:59.882
be able to empathize with ocean life?
I thought that was a cool idea.

00:03:59.882 --> 00:04:01.262
The big question for me is how long

00:04:01.262 --> 00:04:04.232
does that effect last, right? So I would love

00:04:04.232 --> 00:04:07.561
to see more research that shows how long

00:04:07.561 --> 00:04:11.542
what happens in VR lingers in your real life.

